[2026-05-07T14:09:49.765Z] [INFO] 2026-05-07T14:09:49.764974Z DEBUG session_loop{thread_id=019e02c1-ebdc-7ef2-b71f-0d6034cbaac1}:submission_dispatch{otel.name="op.dispatch.user_input_with_turn_context" submission.id="019e02c1-ec33-7e11-ae1d-3c59f6f85260" codex.op="user_input_with_turn_context"}:turn{otel.name="session_task.turn" thread.id=019e02c1-ebdc-7ef2-b71f-0d6034cbaac1 turn.id=019e02c1-ec33-7e11-ae1d-3c59f6f85260 model=gpt-5.5}: codex_core::stream_events_utils: Output item item=Reasoning { id: "rs_069d6ee734aa1e8c0169fc9d231938819198dc1dcc42789be0", summary: [SummaryText { text: "**Considering newline handling**\n\nI'm thinking about handling trailing newlines and whether `apply_patch` can remove them. It seems to always leave them intact. I wonder if using Perl or shell editing would work. The developer mentioned using `apply_patch` for manual code edits, but to remove newlines, maybe I should try `perl -0pi` or even `git checkout origin/main -- .gitkeep`. But I recall the caution against destructive commands unless asked. So, `perl` may be the way to go, as it allows for mechanical rewriting without using `cat`." }, SummaryText { text: "**Exploring newline removal methods**\n\nI'm considering different ways to handle that pesky newline at the end of `.gitkeep`. I could use `truncate -s -1 .gitkeep` as a minimal shell trick to remove it. There's also the option to use `git restore --source=origin/main .gitkeep`, but I want to avoid that if possible. Since `apply_patch` can't handle the final newline, perhaps `perl -0pi -e 's/\\n\\z//' .gitkeep` is a solid choice. Staging the change will be necessary too." }], content: None, encrypted_content: Some("gAAAAABp_J0tgaeND3ik3PJA1FUoU7piVemw3XbfI06IYjklK_Ow5cHrCJh2TXNvP26TgE4yDUy4jqQCir4VDbridoMbSjKZEyFqnf5CODAKhoER1EjKLWkUGiZdwFIQRmFI74nuY5zkmgXbmDv961vP_iqeXvGWCRqQaixePVDuQg2vif9iDKDD8UTZPQbXT9J2FxKF_cbwhTQxuMQq4QuhN7hwTvwMWWP55ZlWVrWxXct7E8yBnWx_MULQ_2ya96bUGwbxJr6Zh7z7vOt3mzRrnMFISzgaRxCpo-HjnwyNTVbNiK-rArZ0Z0TM