[2026-05-07T14:09:49.766Z] [INFO] {"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_107","type":"reasoning","text":"**Considering newline handling**\n\nI'm thinking about handling trailing newlines and whether `apply_patch` can remove them. It seems to always leave them intact. I wonder if using Perl or shell editing would work. The developer mentioned using `apply_patch` for manual code edits, but to remove newlines, maybe I should try `perl -0pi` or even `git checkout origin/main -- .gitkeep`. But I recall the caution against destructive commands unless asked. So, `perl` may be the way to go, as it allows for mechanical rewriting without using `cat`.\n**Exploring newline removal methods**\n\nI'm considering different ways to handle that pesky newline at the end of `.gitkeep`. I could use `truncate -s -1 .gitkeep` as a minimal shell trick to remove it. There's also the option to use `git restore --source=origin/main .gitkeep`, but I want to avoid that if possible. Since `apply_patch` can't handle the final newline, perhaps `perl -0pi -e 's/\\n\\z//' .gitkeep` is a solid choice. Staging the change will be necessary too."}}
